{:edb/db-before-t 2452 :edb/db-after-t 2453 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535765 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:47:11.937Z" 13194139535765 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365227 13194139535765 true] [17592186221352 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365234 13194139535765 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365200 13194139535765 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365207 13194139535765 true] [17592186221353 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365214 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365201 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1089 "q-333365" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1100 "A small perturbation away from an equilibrium causes trajectories to slowly spiral towards the equilibrium. How should the equilibrium be classified?" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1113 #uuid "638a5026-be36-5dd1-908b-d2c6d418484f" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365200 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Asymptotically stable.\"" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365202 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365203 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365204 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365205 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365206 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1041 "selection" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365204 13194139535765 true] [17592186365201 1110 #uuid "0f82aee8-df8d-5697-a473-6e7ab9e13456" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365202 1034 "Unstable." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535765 true] [17592186365202 1111 #uuid "dfc6b856-4f58-5f40-bbc1-d8c18ae5096a" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365203 1034 "Asymptotically unstable." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535765 true] [17592186365203 1111 #uuid "767723fb-d32d-5a40-8828-30177dc2a0cd" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365204 1034 "Asymptotically stable." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535765 true] [17592186365204 1111 #uuid "e019e31f-ebc1-5bab-a719-499a4ea439c1" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365205 1034 "Stable but not asymptotically stable." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535765 true] [17592186365205 1111 #uuid "16a59d8a-69e3-52c5-aa62-d8170533b658" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365206 1034 "Asymptotically stable only for nonlinear systems." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535765 true] [17592186365206 1111 #uuid "befa8d07-e937-5866-bf06-12a6368ff3f4" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365208 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1089 "q-335454" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1100 "Which statement correctly describes an **unstable** equilibrium point?" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1113 #uuid "894667d1-f4aa-5975-a79e-d7ee80410cd6" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365207 1145 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\nSome solutions starting arbitrarily close to the equilibrium eventually leave a fixed neighborhood of it." 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365209 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365210 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365211 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365212 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365213 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1041 "selection" 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365210 13194139535765 true] [17592186365208 1